1
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:22,890
 Galaxy real seas bound on cosmic paths we'll trace.

2
00:01:16,390 --> 00:01:32,210
 It's such a wondrous thing.

3
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:35,680
 Be hot to solve.

4
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:39,300
 Come see, come take your place.

5
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:40,900
 We're leaving soon.

6
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:22,650
 Just astronauts with diplomas to prove it.

7
00:02:24,130 --> 00:02:26,160
 Very hard at cadet school.

8
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:27,420
 You both deserve a vacation.

9
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:30,090
 Vacation is what we want right now.

10
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:33,180
 Yeah, a nice space-type punch-up would be just great, right?

11
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:54,850
 I'm a man's job.

12
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:57,950
 I'll be the first to admit it.

13
00:03:58,010 --> 00:04:00,630
 Your P.R.O. is head and shoulders above that clumsy O.F.

14
00:04:01,470 --> 00:04:04,440
 Out at the top of this cadet class.

15
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:05,500
 Oh, that doesn't mean a lot.

16
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:09,720
 Unidentified object, entering field of observation, position 10 o'clock.

17
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,490
 Here it comes again. What is this anyway?

18
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:12,750
 To the control room.

19
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:18,900
 Unidentified object, position 11 o'clock, direction northeast.

20
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:24,240
 Can't get a precise scan of the object. It's moving at a high velocity.

21
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:25,560
 Can't identify what it is.

22
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:28,330
 Such a high speed tells us it's most unusual, Colonel.

23
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:30,290
 The truth is, we don't understand.

24
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:35,390
 Unidentified object, leaving field of observation, position 11 hours, direction due north.

25
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:38,870
 Return to duty stations. We'll notify the council immediately.

26
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:40,550
 Don't worry, Maestro, we'll figure it out.

27
00:04:40,550 --> 00:05:03,840
 Wait, it was one of my offices in space space warp.

28
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:06,440
 How did you win? It's me who programmed you.

29
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:10,440
 Elementary, you are distracted, and I have one-track mind.

30
00:05:10,590 --> 00:05:19,610
 He's given me an idea.

31
00:05:39,250 --> 00:05:48,930
 Our field here, then vanished.

32
00:05:49,570 --> 00:05:54,710
 We don't know much, but our radar indicates that it's either an asteroid deflected out of orbit or a gigantic spaceship.

33
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:56,610
 It's moving mighty quick for a spaceship.

34
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:59,930
 There isn't one of our systems that can get a ship up to that speed.

35
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:02,960
 Pretty certain that ship isn't from our galaxies.

36
00:06:03,550 --> 00:06:05,440
 Look, I've plotted its trajectory.

37
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:08,050
 It might have come from some very distant region.

38
00:06:08,290 --> 00:06:10,860
 Here, for instance, from the great cloud of Horus.

39
00:06:11,150 --> 00:06:11,870
 What do you make of it?

40
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:14,290
 Quite a ways all the same.

41
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:17,130
 How'd they get through those millions of light years?

42
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:18,450
 It can be done.

43
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:20,310
 We're not back in the 20th century.

44
00:06:20,950 --> 00:06:24,530
 Ever since we set up our interstellar patrol, a lot has been cleared up.

45
00:06:24,850 --> 00:06:31,080
 In our time, there are many examples of theoretical exploits that were impossible only to be achieved in time.

46
00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:33,300
 I'm willing to reserve all judgment on it.

47
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:34,210
 I agree.

48
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:36,000
 But that poses one question.

49
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:38,180
 Is this alien ship a friend or an enemy?

50
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:39,660
 That is the question.

51
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:46,240
 Yes, that is the question, and I won't deny it's an important one.

52
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:54,520
 The trajectory is much more that of a spacecraft and a meteor.

53
00:06:54,740 --> 00:06:56,820
 It commands speeds superior to any of our craft.

54
00:06:56,930 --> 00:06:59,300
 But to make of it, nothing prevents our imagining.

55
00:06:59,580 --> 00:07:04,460
 Somewhere out in space, there can be a civilization that's technically in advance beyond us.

56
00:07:04,810 --> 00:07:05,350
 It's a spaceship.

57
00:07:06,530 --> 00:07:09,450
 Well, that would confirm the colonel's report.

58
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:12,020
 But an important question remains.

59
00:07:12,330 --> 00:07:13,540
 Is it friendly or not?

60
00:07:13,830 --> 00:07:15,750
 I rather doubt that it's an enemy.

61
00:07:16,270 --> 00:07:18,080
 Can you explain why you hold that opinion?

62
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:25,580
 I can't explain it. The feeling, I don't know why.

63
00:07:26,180 --> 00:07:28,400
 Very persuasive argument to give the council.

64
00:07:29,130 --> 00:07:31,290
 Thank you, my friend. I know you are very busy.

65
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:47,690
 Go on. We must wait as long as we are not subjected to any hostile action from the ship.

66
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:51,210
 I hope all of you agree with the opinion of my government.

67
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:55,800
 Namely, we feel it is our distinct duty to stand ready to respond with determination,

68
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:58,260
 but it is also our duty to remain calm.

69
00:07:58,600 --> 00:07:59,960
 Oh dear, what was I saying?

70
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:03,520
 Determination, but also our duty to remain calm.

71
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:07,880
 Ah, yes. That is the fundamental principle of our confederation. The reason for our existence.

72
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:19,140
 Chief, you're 100% right. Quite right.

73
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:21,960
 I want an emergency session of the full council.

74
00:08:22,190 --> 00:08:26,010
 I'm not going to allow any foreign force to violate our territory in this land.

75
00:08:26,010 --> 00:08:30,190
 Yes, I understand. I really do 100%, Chief. But what if the council won't agree?

76
00:08:30,310 --> 00:08:36,780
 You shut up! They'll agree. I want it. I want an immediate reprisal. Now wait!

77
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:42,790
 I know, Chief. Just as you say, Chief. Right, right.

78
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:53,330
 Morning, Madam President.

79
00:08:53,330 --> 00:09:00,450
 Now to work, gentlemen. Will you all take your places?

80
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:06,220
 Due to the exceptional situation, two distinguished leaders will join our council today, we are told.

81
00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:08,530
 The two should be here soon.

82
00:09:08,530 --> 00:09:22,610
 The leaders of Cassiopeia and of Scorpio. If you please, welcome to this special session, Dr. Newman, distinguished leader of the constellation Scorpio.

83
00:09:22,610 --> 00:09:29,830
 Here I am. I'm here. Sorry to be late. Am I late? I am. You're all here.

84
00:09:29,830 --> 00:09:35,360
 Sorry. A bit of work to finish up. You know how it is. Perhaps you don't know how it is.

85
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:37,720
 Ah, Professor, how are you, dear colleague?

86
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:39,760
 It's been light years since we last met.

87
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:40,620
 I was wondering.

88
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:44,590
 I didn't realize.

89
00:09:44,770 --> 00:09:47,420
 Something I hate is a hologram.

90
00:09:47,980 --> 00:09:49,600
 Well, council meeting is now in session.

91
00:09:50,030 --> 00:09:51,090
 Wait one microsecond.

92
00:09:51,190 --> 00:09:52,030
 Another leader is coming.

93
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:58,770
 Acknowledge the presence among us of the chief of the Constellation, Cassapia, General Grouchy.

94
00:09:58,970 --> 00:09:59,990
 It's crazy.

95
00:10:00,390 --> 00:10:03,950
 We're not going to let some alien violate our territory like he owned it.

96
00:10:04,170 --> 00:10:05,930
 This is our region, and it's in danger.

97
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:10,730
 So we're hitting hard, teach him a good lesson, and nobody else will come in here thumbing his nose at us.

98
00:10:10,990 --> 00:10:12,470
 Hooray! Well said, my chief.

99
00:10:13,590 --> 00:10:20,200
 Representative of Andromeda will speak now. Please, gentlemen, silence for the next speaker.

100
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:27,070
 Total agreement with the general.

101
00:10:28,150 --> 00:10:31,530
 What do we do? We don't even know the force in question.

102
00:10:31,770 --> 00:10:35,790
 We have no idea where the spaceships come from.

103
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:38,440
 Yes, we must find out exactly why it's come.

104
00:10:38,930 --> 00:10:41,930
 May I repeat that my government still maintains,

105
00:10:41,930 --> 00:10:46,250
 so long as we cannot report any form of open hostility on the part of the object,

106
00:10:46,590 --> 00:10:48,770
 Our only option is establishing contact with it.

107
00:10:49,150 --> 00:10:52,090
 Need I remind you that that is the fundamental policy of our confederation.

108
00:10:52,130 --> 00:10:56,130
 Yes, and we wait here quietly till those nice guys blow us out of the universe.

109
00:10:56,130 --> 00:10:58,890
 My dear friend, if this unknown spaceship is armed for war,

110
00:10:58,970 --> 00:11:02,270
 then the force of its weapon is probably beyond all measure to any of ours,

111
00:11:02,350 --> 00:11:04,290
 and so there's no question of our winning.

112
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:07,460
 Do as you want, but I'm ready to fight them all along.

113
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:08,740
 But this is madness.

114
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:11,320
 Any overt attack in it would mean total war.

115
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:15,740
 Yes, even if you did manage to destroy this ship, hundreds more would come.

116
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:18,570
 Why not take a vote right now? It's the best way.

117
00:11:18,830 --> 00:11:19,770
 Yes, why not?

118
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:21,040
 Make your minds up.

119
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:22,560
 We will now proceed to a vote.

120
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:23,880
 The rule of the majority.

121
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:28,480
 All those voting for attacking the spacecraft if it refuses to identify itself.

122
00:11:31,330 --> 00:11:36,710
 Votes.

123
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:41,640
 Now all those voting to make contact and refrain from any use of force pending investigation.

124
00:11:45,780 --> 00:11:51,910
 One extension.

125
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:54,940
 I vote for the second proposition.

126
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:58,720
 Hence, we agree to adopt that resolution by six votes.

127
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:03,000
 That is, an absolute majority of the 11 members of the Confederation.

128
00:12:07,210 --> 00:12:09,590
 Well, perfect.

129
00:12:10,350 --> 00:12:12,170
 Well, how do you think?

130
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:13,920
 Looks very good on you, admittedly.

131
00:12:14,090 --> 00:12:15,420
 Trifle old-fashioned, isn't it?

132
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:16,730
 Not at all.

133
00:12:16,890 --> 00:12:18,050
 Makes you look quite sporty.

134
00:12:18,290 --> 00:12:20,970
 I suspect that your opinion is not entirely objective.

135
00:12:21,890 --> 00:12:25,280
 I am the one who's the scientific genius, huh?

136
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:26,990
 You don't seem convinced.

137
00:12:27,150 --> 00:12:28,210
 Well, say something.

138
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:31,440
 Oh, well, really, who put together this junk pile?

139
00:12:31,640 --> 00:12:36,990
 I would suggest, Master, that you give rather more consideration to the expression you use in point of fact.

140
00:12:36,990 --> 00:12:39,490
 You are the one who made this junk pile, as you say.

141
00:12:41,390 --> 00:12:41,890
 Right.

142
00:12:43,380 --> 00:12:44,690
 Oh, I did.

143
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:47,800
 You are my pride and joy, my old friend.

144
00:12:47,980 --> 00:12:48,920
 Yes, you are.

145
00:12:49,860 --> 00:12:54,900
 With friends like you, who needs enemies, will you kindly put me together?

146
00:12:55,210 --> 00:12:56,410
 I am feeling quite new.

147
00:12:56,950 --> 00:12:58,610
 Spacecraft Cosmopolitan approaching.

148
00:12:59,460 --> 00:13:01,300
 Unprescribed course. End of message.

149
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:05,280
 You hear that now? You must really excuse me.

150
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:06,500
 The children are coming.

151
00:13:06,820 --> 00:13:08,460
 The children, you know.

152
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:11,000
 The children at last.

153
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:19,140
 She's not as young as I used to be, man.

154
00:13:19,260 --> 00:13:21,900
 Hey, wait, don't leave without...

155
00:13:21,900 --> 00:13:22,760
 Wait, wait, wait.

156
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:27,280
 Oh, my straw is last.

157
00:13:28,320 --> 00:14:13,250
 She's a friend.

158
00:14:13,730 --> 00:14:14,790
 We arrived on the same spaceship.

159
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:16,660
 Father's stationed in the control room.

160
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:17,160
 Psyche?

161
00:14:17,450 --> 00:14:18,820
 It's a curious name.

162
00:14:19,250 --> 00:14:21,710
 Her real name is Mercedes, but we call her Psyche.

163
00:14:22,010 --> 00:14:23,210
 She has special powers.

164
00:14:23,600 --> 00:14:24,430
 Yes, she does.

165
00:14:24,710 --> 00:14:26,230
 In fact, she's a bit of a witch, you'll see.

166
00:14:28,710 --> 00:14:41,100
 As Chief of the Confederation's Intergalactic Police Force, I would like to welcome you all.

167
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:43,560
 Your ambitions are to have careers in the police.

168
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:46,700
 Let me outline the stringent program you have before you.

169
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:51,260
 Your role, and never forget it, will be above all, furnishing assistance when it is required.

170
00:14:51,660 --> 00:14:54,400
 Assistance to spacecraft of our allies whenever in difficulty.

171
00:14:54,940 --> 00:15:00,450
 Exploration, discovering unknown galaxies, in-depth probes to learn more about new worlds

172
00:15:00,450 --> 00:15:02,990
 and whether they are ready to join our Confederation.

173
00:15:02,990 --> 00:15:05,930
 All this and more will become your duties.

174
00:15:06,170 --> 00:15:11,610
 This planet, Omega, is the most advanced outpost for all our diverse and complementary civilizations.

175
00:15:12,070 --> 00:15:14,390
 Our galactic police are its guardians and its symbol.

176
00:15:14,750 --> 00:15:16,770
 I know you will all be worthy of your high calling.

177
00:15:17,070 --> 00:15:19,750
 We will see each other often. I will watch your progress with interest.

178
00:15:20,490 --> 00:15:24,910
 I will leave you now to continue your course with your eminent professor, Maestro.

179
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:38,910
 You see, speaking, no, the fact is I can't see anything at all from back there.

180
00:15:39,810 --> 00:15:43,490
 You will all travel in space, in hyperspace.

181
00:15:43,490 --> 00:15:47,530
 Of course, you all know that a light year is the distance light travels in one year.

182
00:15:48,250 --> 00:15:50,910
 Yes, well, I suppose I shouldn't have said that. You know that already.

183
00:15:51,170 --> 00:15:54,250
 Now, the speed of light is 300,000 kilometers per second.

184
00:15:54,660 --> 00:15:57,830
 Let's see. That ought to be around 18 million...

185
00:15:57,830 --> 00:16:00,670
 No, it's more likely the round sum of what...

186
00:16:00,670 --> 00:16:08,650
 It's quite so. Exactly what I was just about to say.

187
00:16:08,730 --> 00:16:11,670
 9,460,800,000,000 kilometers a year.

188
00:16:11,910 --> 00:16:13,670
 All the same, that is a lot.

189
00:16:13,790 --> 00:16:15,490
 You know, I can hardly believe it's true.

190
00:16:15,910 --> 00:16:16,710
 Now, where was I?

191
00:16:16,810 --> 00:16:18,550
 I guess we'd better start all over again.

192
00:16:18,870 --> 00:16:20,290
 You know, the planet Astarte.

193
00:16:20,750 --> 00:16:23,450
 It is located at a distance of 50 million parsecs.

194
00:16:23,510 --> 00:16:26,490
 A parsec is, as you know, 3.26 light years.

195
00:16:26,910 --> 00:16:31,390
 You're familiar with Einstein's theory and the curvature of space, time, and Fitzgerald's theory of contraction.

196
00:16:32,070 --> 00:16:32,720
 Be good.

197
00:16:32,980 --> 00:16:35,840
 Now, let us calculate the time it would take a space...

198
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:39,420
 You know, Pierre, I'm still wondering what's behind General Gratul's aggressive tactics.

199
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:41,400
 By now, you must be getting used to him.

200
00:16:41,810 --> 00:16:43,460
 I assure you, it's no joking matter.

201
00:16:43,540 --> 00:16:44,520
 It's a serious situation.

202
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:47,440
 All of his craft are faster than any others in the Confederation.

203
00:16:47,760 --> 00:16:51,180
 Not only that, he has more spacecraft than anyone else, and they're better armed.

204
00:16:51,560 --> 00:16:53,440
 We've always been careful with him. Why?

205
00:16:53,750 --> 00:16:55,860
 We want the status quo and the general calm.

206
00:16:56,180 --> 00:16:59,360
 But you know his ambitions and that of Andromeda right behind him.

207
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:02,200
 Whether they like him or not, they're with him because they're scared of him.

208
00:17:02,880 --> 00:17:06,680
 Yeah, okay, but right now what matters is finding out about that alien ship.

209
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:07,720
 The general can wait.

210
00:17:10,590 --> 00:17:14,990
 Unidentified object entering field of observation position one o'clock direction west to southwest.

211
00:17:15,320 --> 00:17:16,010
 It's here again.

212
00:17:16,010 --> 00:17:23,110
 Zoom in tight and definition set at maximum.

213
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:30,490
 Now return to standard display.

214
00:17:32,410 --> 00:17:35,830
 Unidentified object at 12 hours 40, direction west-southwest.

215
00:17:38,860 --> 00:17:43,840
 Okay, operation underway. Don't lose any time.

216
00:17:44,140 --> 00:17:45,510
 The ship, Hummingbird.

217
00:17:45,820 --> 00:17:48,360
 She's our fastest. Its equipment should be adequate.

218
00:17:48,650 --> 00:17:49,870
 Who do you see commanding her?

219
00:17:50,240 --> 00:17:51,070
 You think.

220
00:17:51,770 --> 00:17:53,080
 You command, then.

221
00:17:53,260 --> 00:17:55,070
 But remember, you're on a contact mission.

222
00:17:55,350 --> 00:17:57,590
 Absolutely no hostile action, no matter what. That's my order.

223
00:17:57,880 --> 00:17:58,700
 Yes, sir.

224
00:17:59,260 --> 00:18:01,890
 That's your interception point.

225
00:18:02,330 --> 00:18:04,070
 Have your orders. Carry them out.

226
00:18:05,210 --> 00:18:07,870
 This is your commander.

227
00:18:07,870 --> 00:18:14,820
 space cruiser hummingbird in launch bay number four crew at your posts immediately departure in

228
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:30,540
 four minutes 30 seconds out leave go ahead and don't forget what my chief said it's like their

229
00:18:30,540 --> 00:18:36,560
 father's pop why can't we go with you on this mission he's right why can't we go along oh come

230
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:44,820
 on dad come on why not let us see how it's done right theory we've had that up to here all right

231
00:18:44,820 --> 00:18:46,380
 Then it'll be an educational trip.

232
00:18:46,720 --> 00:18:47,020
 Yeah!

233
00:18:47,930 --> 00:19:44,560
 Reporting, Command.

234
00:19:46,250 --> 00:19:47,820
 Charlie 3, reporting, Commander.

235
00:19:48,170 --> 00:19:49,430
 Oxygen and pressure okay.

236
00:19:49,550 --> 00:19:50,270
 Ready for liftoff.

237
00:19:50,710 --> 00:19:52,340
 Everybody at your stations.

238
00:19:54,530 --> 00:19:58,730
 Parcher in 30 seconds.

239
00:20:02,210 --> 00:20:05,190
 Seats automatically secured.

240
00:20:05,530 --> 00:20:05,860
 Ready.

241
00:20:06,790 --> 00:20:08,830
 Minus 16 seconds.

242
00:20:09,050 --> 00:20:09,290
 Mark.

243
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:10,650
 Program.

244
00:20:11,050 --> 00:20:11,550
 Countdown.

245
00:20:11,940 --> 00:20:12,440
 In operation.

246
00:20:14,070 --> 00:20:19,910
 10, 9, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3.

247
00:20:20,600 --> 00:20:21,580
 Ignition.

248
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:23,180
